  "answer": "<p><b>Leveraging Amazon Compute Optimizer</b> is one of the best ways to reduce AWS costs effectively. This service analyzes your resource consumption patterns and offers recommendations for right-sizing your EC2 instances. By implementing its advice, you can remove over-provisioned resources, paying only for what you need, without risking performance or availability.<br><br>Additionally, <b>shutting down non-production EC2 instances on weekends or after work hours</b> is a highly practical method to avoid unnecessary spend. Many development or testing workloads don’t require 24/7 availability—turning these resources off can significantly reduce costs with zero impact on end-user service.<br><br>The other options are less suitable for the following reasons:<br><br><i>Switching from On-Demand to Spot Instances for Jenkins managing production CI/CD pipelines</i> is risky, because Spot Instances can be interrupted with little notice, risking failed builds or deploys at critical moments—this can directly impact business operations.<br><br><i>Using Direct Connect instead of VPN</i> does provide higher reliability and performance, but it is generally a more expensive solution rather than a cheaper one, especially for smaller workloads or companies without a consistent large data transfer need.<br><br><i>Choosing gp2 SSDs over Throughput Optimized HDDs (st1) in non-production</i> environments is misguided since storage selection should align with workload needs: st1 volumes are often cheaper but are designed for high-throughput requirements, while gp2 volumes are optimized for IOPS. Blindly switching without workload analysis can actually increase costs or degrade performance.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> Focus on matching resource type and size to workload, use automation to control resource schedules, and always verify that cost-saving actions don't jeopardize business-critical workloads.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your client, a large AWS consumer, is concerned about rising AWS bills. They've requested your expert recommendations for strategies to keep AWS spending in check, without affecting the availability or performance of their existing cloud workloads.<br>Which of the following tactics would you advise them to follow? (Select TWO options.)</p>",
